00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.280 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to welcome to the program  today Catherine eben who is an  investigative journalist and Fortune  magazine contributor as well as the  author of bottle of lies the inside  story of the generic drug boom Catherine  so great to talk to you today thank you  so much for having me on the show  so generic drugs is an issue that  relates to intellectual property it  relates to FDA regulation it relates to  the medical world I mean there's so many  different areas that are relevant to  talking about generic drugs but I guess  to begin with how far back does the  concept of a generic go well I mean it  really goes all the way back to probably  the 1920's 1930's but the idea that you  could you could basically be a company  that breaks down a brand-name drug and  tries to remake some version of it is a  very old concept in the sort of modern  generic drug world when we look at  generic alternatives to name-brand drugs  many people believe that it is down to  the finest level the exact same thing as  the brand name except without the brand  name it turns out that that that's not  always true and it's in fact often not  true in ways that are sort of concerning  and dangerous talk a little bit about  that right well it's actually almost  never true  so generics are not identical copies  they are versions of brand-name drugs  and the FDA recognizes that they're not  identical because they give a range that  the generic can fall into in terms of  the dissolution of the drug in the blood  so under the best of circumstances if  the generic is similar but not identical  they may have different active different  additional ingredients different time  release mechanism they're made in  different factories there  dissolved differently in the body but  where it really gets dangerous and what  I documented my book bottle of lies is  the ways in which generic drug  manufacturing plants many of which  operate overseas are actually committing  fraud and submitting fake data to get  approval from the FDA to sell drugs  right so there's sort of two layers to  this one is is there even the  expectation or belief that the generic  is in practical terms the same thing as  the name brand drug then separately for  that from that there's the question of  oversight and accuracy as to whether the  generic is even what it purports to be  and it seems as though both of these are  issues that are happening at the same  time right I mean the the idea of the  generic as a version and not an  identical copy is accepted and actually  acceptable and for many many drugs a  version is fine although there are some  drugs where really precise dosing is  required and those can be a problem but  the I think the greater risk for  American consumers is that the FDA is  not adequately policing these  manufacturing plants overseas and  essentially accepting the data that  these companies are submitting as  accurate when they are not and it's not  always I mean so we can there are a  number of criticisms of the FDA many of  which I've made on this program but is  this limitation in terms of the  standards that are being applied to  generics is this something that the FDA  is choosing not to do or is it something  that it is at least in some cases unable  to do well I think the two are somewhat  connected but basically the FDA is a  domestic agency that because of  globalization was suddenly required to  police manufacturing plants all over the  world and it essentially acknowledges  the difficulty of this because for u.s.  plants when they inspects  they just the inspectors arrive on an  unannounced and stay as long as is  needed but because of the complex  logistics of overseas inspections the  FDA  says that it's arriving to these plants  gives them weeks and sometimes months of  advance notice that they're coming  so my investigation reveals they will  send in the plants will send in data  fabrication teams to alter documents  invent documents back date documents and  these are all you know quality data  quality documents that speak to whether  the drug is bioequivalent whether it's  got impurities in it or not all kinds of  issues that are critical to have the  drug functions when we think about this  there might be people depending on their  health circumstances who here is talking  about this and they go to Advil versus  the CVS generic equivalent for example  but there are other people who may be in  situations where they are actually using  generic alternatives to very very  expensive name-brand drugs depending on  the the medical conditions that they may  have does your investigation suggest  that the same issues exist to the same  degree with both the advil generics and  the really expensive drugs for less  common conditions or is it more one  versus the other it's actually both so  just to give you an example in the book  I follow these cardiologists from the  Cleveland Clinic who realized that when  their patients were getting switched to  certain generics they were initially  stable and then they were no longer  stable and even they found that heart  transplant patients were suffering organ  rejection when they were switched to  certain Indian generics so there is a  that's generic drugs that were  manufactured in India so there's you  know life-threatening dangers here my  book focuses less on over-the-counter  drugs but you know what consumers should  know is that the drugstore brand say  Duane Reade CBS the drugstore chains are  contracting with the lowest-cost Indian  manufacturers to make those  over-the-counter drugs and those plants  are really audited and in  affected infrequently so there is a  quality issue there as well so I mean  having dug into this and investigated it  for the period of time that you did to  write the book is your takeaway that  there's just too much risk with any  generic and that patients should be  doing everything they can to get their  insurance to cover the name-brand  because I will talk about the sort of  practical limitations of that I mean is  it are you did you come away that  concerned where you would just want the  name-brand of any drug that you are  taking I would say two things about that  I came away extremely concerned and I  interviewed plenty of FDA investigators  who themselves would not take the drugs  made into plants that they were  inspecting but that said I also  recognize cost is a serious reality and  and it is for me as well so what I do is  I often ask my pharmacist to switch from  one generic which I don't trust to  another generic made by a different  company that I might trust more that so  it's you know it's it's knowable which  brands generics are more or less is is  trustworthy even the right word  it is a knowable thing now the problem  is do you have to be an investigative  journalist who's looked into this for  ten years to know it right on in order  to help consumers I have put information  on my website which is Kathryn eben comm  and so there is information about steps  that they can take to try to figure out  whether they're generic is trustworthy  or not and what the options are if they  determine that they want to switch you  know it is a bit of a maze I'm not gonna  be you know kind of sugarcoat that I  think what's really needed is  congressional action here to fix the  problems that I've exposed but in lieu  of that I think there are certain steps  that consumers can take there's also  something called an authorised generic  so that is a generic where the brand  company  has made a contract with a generic  company handed over its recipe and the  generic company is making the drug under  a contract so it's actually closer to  the brand and sometimes those authorized  generics are actually manufactured in  the very same plans as the brand-name  drug what what you know a lot of people  who have dealt with insurance companies  know is that depending on the coverage  that you have and depending on where you  get your insurance whether an individual  marketplace as a self-employed person or  through a work connected insurance plan  there are all sorts of complex rules  about what your insurance will or will  not cover when it comes to prescription  medications and doctors certainly have  the ability to put no substitution on  prescriptions which effectively tells  the pharmacy that a name-brand is what  should be provided but that does not  guarantee that health insurance is going  to cover the name-brand at the same  price that the generic would be covered  so this is I mean this is really  complicated stuff particularly for  people who require medications that are  extremely expensive absolutely this is  not simple and you're quite right that  even with the do not substitute there's  no guarantee that the insurance company  will cover a brand when a generic is  available can you talk a little bit  about I mean obviously there are a  number of different types of concerns  there there's straight-up safety  concerns right of side-effects that one  could experience experience adverse  reactions there's also the concern of  just a drug that in its generic version  is less potent or may not achieve the  same results as the name-brand how would  you sort of put percentages on those two  concerns and others that may exist as a  general rule when we talk about ways in  which the generics may be suboptimal  right so I don't know that I can offer  percentages what I can say is that even  if the generics are fine there is an  issue for what it are called narrow  therapeutic index drugs and so those are  drugs that require really precise dosing  and the doctors who prescribe those so  it's cardiologists like  trysts endocrinologists they are  familiar with this as a problem and they  have raised questions about whether the  FDA standards are narrow enough to make  to ensure that the generics are  clinically equivalent to the brand so  that's like in one basket of concerns  unfortunately with this other basket  there is really widespread fraud in the  overseas manufacturing plants that are  making the majority of our generics so  just as an example I feature in the book  an FDA investigator named Peter Baker  who became exceptionally good at finding  data fraud in these plants and over five  years he investigated 86 plants in India  and China and found some element of data  fraud or data deception in about  four-fifths of those plants so a  stunning number yeah it really is yeah  absolutely so I mean these are these are  plants that are doing everything from  using the brand-name drugs to run tests  and take that data and submit it to the  FDA as though it was their own data from  their own drugs so completely  supplanting the data inventing documents  and steaming them in rooms overnight to  make them look old you know completely  just discarding critical manufacturing  documents where FDA investigators have  dug them out of dumpsters when documents  are supposed to be retained so you know  these are really major lapses in what  are called good manufacturing practices  that these plants have to follow I mean  some plants have even wouldn't you have  a sterile manufacturing plant and that's  very serious they have to follow sort of  aseptic regulations um they have  literally invented their sterility data  they're supposed to test air water  surfaces to make sure they're free of  microbial contaminants and they've just  invented that data they're not really  running the tests so that's the kind of  things that are going on in these  planets absolutely absolutely stunning  make sure to check out Catherine's book  bottle of lies the inside story of the  generic drug boom we have been speaking  with the book's author Catherine eben  who is an investigative journalist and  Fortune magazine contributor Catherine  so great to talk to you about this great  to talk to you thank you so much

